1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,955
Episode Sixty-Six wrapped JVM interview answers — heap, GC, JIT, and measurement.

2
00:00:05,955 --> 00:00:12,572
Architecture interviews shift next — from how the JVM runs code to how you structure code.

3
00:00:12,572 --> 00:00:18,233
Design patterns are reusable solutions to recurring software design problems.

4
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:23,968
They are not libraries — they are named ideas teams use to communicate intent.

5
00:00:23,968 --> 00:00:30,290
Junior engineers memorize names — seniors know when a pattern helps and when it hurts.

6
00:00:30,290 --> 00:00:36,466
Today — what patterns are, the three GoF categories, and how Java already uses them.

7
00:00:36,466 --> 00:00:38,425
Episode Sixty-Seven.

8
00:00:38,425 --> 00:00:40,580
Design Patterns Intro.

9
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:44,458
A design pattern names a proven structure for a common problem.

10
00:00:44,458 --> 00:00:49,259
The Gang of Four book cataloged twenty-three classic object-oriented patterns.

11
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:53,568
Each pattern has a problem, a structure, consequences, and known uses.

12
00:00:53,568 --> 00:00:58,430
Patterns create a shared vocabulary — say Adapter and teammates know the shape.

13
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:02,554
They are tools for clarity — not badges to sprinkle on every class.

14
00:01:02,554 --> 00:01:08,094
Prefer the simplest design that communicates intent — patterns when complexity earns them.

15
00:01:08,094 --> 00:01:11,212
Patterns group into three categories by intent.

16
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:16,188
Creational — control how objects are created — Singleton, Factory, Builder.

17
00:01:16,188 --> 00:01:21,296
Structural — compose classes and objects — Adapter, Decorator, Facade, Proxy.

18
00:01:21,296 --> 00:01:26,669
Behavioral — organize communication and algorithms — Strategy, Observer, Command.

19
00:01:26,669 --> 00:01:31,645
Episodes Sixty-Eight through Seventy walk each category with Java examples.

20
00:01:31,645 --> 00:01:36,952
Category first — then pick the pattern that matches the force you are balancing.

21
00:01:36,952 --> 00:01:40,941
Java's standard library is full of patterns you already use.

22
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:45,596
Iterator — for-each loops walk collections without exposing structure.

23
00:01:45,596 --> 00:01:50,117
Observer — listeners and reactive streams notify interested parties.

24
00:01:50,117 --> 00:01:55,702
Decorator — java.io streams wrap streams — BufferedInputStream over FileInputStream.

25
00:01:55,702 --> 00:02:00,489
Factory — Calendar.getInstance and Paths.get hide concrete construction.

26
00:02:00,489 --> 00:02:05,210
Recognizing patterns in the JDK trains your eye for application design.

27
00:02:05,210 --> 00:02:09,736
Use a pattern when the problem matches — not when you want a fancy name.

28
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:13,508
Duplicated construction logic — consider Factory or Builder.

29
00:02:13,508 --> 00:02:17,217
Need to swap algorithms at runtime — Strategy fits cleanly.

30
00:02:17,217 --> 00:02:21,241
Legacy API mismatch — Adapter bridges without rewriting callers.

31
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:25,138
If a pattern adds classes without reducing coupling — skip it.

32
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:28,596
Readability for your team beats purity from a textbook.

33
00:02:28,596 --> 00:02:31,013
Pattern abuse is a real failure mode.

34
00:02:31,013 --> 00:02:34,998
Singleton everywhere — hidden global state that breaks tests.

35
00:02:34,998 --> 00:02:39,310
Abstract factory for two concrete types — ceremony without payoff.

36
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:43,295
Observer graphs so tangled that change ripples unpredictably.

37
00:02:43,295 --> 00:02:48,456
Also — renaming a simple method call a Strategy without a family of algorithms.

38
00:02:48,456 --> 00:02:52,702
Patterns serve design — design does not serve pattern checklists.

39
00:02:52,702 --> 00:02:54,125
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:54,125 --> 00:02:58,328
One — memorizing twenty-three names without a problem they solve.

41
00:02:58,328 --> 00:03:01,885
Two — forcing patterns into code that is already clear.

42
00:03:01,885 --> 00:03:06,993
Three — confusing design patterns with architectural styles like microservices.

43
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:11,197
Also — skipping trade-offs — every pattern adds indirection cost.

44
00:03:11,197 --> 00:03:14,624
Name the force, then the pattern — never the reverse.

45
00:03:14,624 --> 00:03:18,731
Interview question — what is a design pattern and why use one?

46
00:03:18,731 --> 00:03:22,507
A named, reusable solution to a recurring design problem.

47
00:03:22,507 --> 00:03:26,548
It improves communication — teams share intent with one word.

48
00:03:26,548 --> 00:03:30,986
It packages trade-offs — you adopt known consequences deliberately.

49
00:03:30,986 --> 00:03:35,226
Java's JDK uses patterns heavily — Iterator, Decorator, Factory.

50
00:03:35,226 --> 00:03:40,194
Use them when they reduce coupling or clarify variation — not for prestige.

51
00:03:40,194 --> 00:03:43,001
Next we go deep on object creation.

52
00:03:43,001 --> 00:03:46,370
Episode Sixty-Eight — Creational Patterns.

53
00:03:46,370 --> 00:03:51,825
Singleton, Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, and Prototype.

54
00:03:51,825 --> 00:03:52,948
See you there.
